From 41a7931a082d0252d59c3c5ff07163e87c5cdbf0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Barnes <hello@jonbarnes.dev>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 04:23:54 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Ray traced shadows on the game's own lighting

Patch 0005 of the renderer series (OoT: Recompiled, .scaffold/upgrades/lighting-design.md).

The shadow pass: one ray per hit pixel toward a point on the sun's disc, placed by blue noise so
neighbors and frames differ, and the result written as a multiplier on the raster color rather
than as a light. The game lit each surface with its ambient plus the sun times the cosine; what
the pass writes is what remains of that with the sun blocked, so shadows sit on the game's own
vertex lighting and a surface the sun does not face keeps its color.

The composite is a factor over the rasterized frame, never a color of its own: the compose
shader writes the shadow's fraction (one where no ray hit) and the post process multiplies the
color target by it. The traced calls are rasterized as well, so the frame the factor multiplies
is the game's, and every raster call keeps its order against them (the pinned renderer's
interleaved rasters are not made: a pre-rendered room draws its depth mesh and then copies the
picture over it, and the copy was being lost).

The sun is the strongest directional light among the game's own, searched across every
projection of the frame because the game sets its lights under the sky's projection before it
draws the world, with that projection's ambient. It is a sun only under a sky: the first
perspective draw of the world's pair is the sky box, depth-free, when there is one, and a room's
directional light traced against the room's own walls shadowed the whole room, so a frame
without a sky has no sun and its interiors are left to the local lights of phase 55. The
parameter block carries the sun with three words of padding before it: the shader aligns a
float4 to sixteen bytes and the CPU side's float4 does not.

The GPU timer: a timestamp pair per span per frame in a query pool per ring slot, read back when
the slot comes round again, and the once a second trace becomes the frame budget line phase 58
reads. The builds are stamped where they are recorded; the shadow dispatch is stamped around the
direct light trace in the framebuffer renderer, the second pinned edit of this patch.

The blue noise table is real: three 64 by 64 void and cluster masks generated offline by
tools/blue_noise.py into a committed header, turned into the 64 slices the shaders index by
frame through a golden ratio step per slice. The interleaved gradient placeholder of patch 0002
is gone.

